  "query_preview": "..Strong high pressure aloft remained centered over \nthe New Mexico/Colorado border Sunday afternoon. As mentioned in \nprevious discussions, the high will wobble in place through\nWednesday before shifting westward to a position centered over\nArizona/Utah by next weekend.\n\nFor today and tonight...Cloud cover suppressed thunderstorm \ncoverage through midday Sunday, especially from a Prescott to \nFour Corners line northward. South of this line sunny skies \nallowing convective development to proceed as normal. We're \nthinking that north of the Prescott to Four corners line shower \nand storm activity will be suppressed to isolated and weak storms.\nSouth of this line, as showers and storm developing over the \nmountains, the flow aloft has shifted enough so the storm motion \nwill be to the west to northwest. This storm motion will inhibit \nany organized line of storms from moving from the Mogollon Rim \nsouth across Gila and Yavapai counties (as they did on Saturday).\nYet still, where it is sunny enough, storms will build over the \nmountains and move off. Storms on the sunny side of the line will \nbe stronger with localized flash flooding possible. In any case, \nactivity will subside by mid evening leaving behind isolated \nshowers and a very slight chance of thunderstorms. With strong \nhigh pressure nearby light winds and hot temperatures continue \nwith an Extreme Heat Warning in effect until 9 PM this evening \nbelow 4000 feet from the Grand Canyon to Page.\n\nFrom Monday into Wednesday...Monsoon moisture will remain\nstationed across northern Arizona. Strong high pressure will \nwobble in place with daily rounds of mountain thunderstorms from \nlate morning into the afternoon then rolling out into the lower \nelevations later in the afternoon into the evening. Each day will\nbe a little different so no sense getting into those details. The\nprimary influence will be that the strong high pressure will \nstart to have a capping/stabilizing influence with low to medium \n(at best) levels of monsoon activity. In addition, temperatures \nwill begin to climb toward Wednesday as high pressure begins to \nshift over Arizona.\n\nFrom Thursday onward...Models insist that the strong high pressure\nsystem over Colorado/New Mexico will shift over Arizona. While \nmonsoon moisture will remain in place, with strong high pressure \ncentered over Arizona we can probably expect low grade monsoon \nactivity, generally of the isolated afternoon and early evening \nvariety. In addition, temperatures are expected to climb further \nwith extreme heat returning to our lower elevation locations.",
